## Decision 001 - Vault Spatial Hash Over Class-Owned NativeMultiHashMap

Problem: The XML asked for `NativeMultiHashMap<int,int>`, but the H-Phi mandate forbids private persistent NativeCollections in the runtime owner. A class-owned hash map would satisfy the algorithm but violate DataVault sovereignty.

Solution: Use vault-owned `ShinobuSpatialHashBucketHeads` and `ShinobuSpatialHashNext` integer buffers. `BuildVaultSpatialHashJob` rebuilds bucket heads every frame and `BurstBoidBehaviorSolverJob` queries 27 neighbor buckets from read-only snapshots.

Rejected Alternatives: A private `NativeMultiHashMap` was rejected because it would create local data ownership. Full all-pairs checks were rejected because 5000 entities produce 25,000,000 distance checks per frame.

Scalability potential: Minimal/Low use the same layout with stress-based boid skipping. Middle/High/Ultra can consume the saved CPU for denser visual fish render payloads through BRG/GraphicsBuffer without changing gameplay truth.

Hardware Impact: Static estimate only. Bounded hash lookup replaces all-pairs work; worst bucket collision is now capped at 64 chain steps per bucket and overflows are visible in telemetry. No profiler microsecond claim is made.

## Decision 002 - Chain Cap and Overflow Telemetry

Problem: The original vault linked-list hash could still degrade if many boids collided into one bucket. The solver had a `guard < Count` condition, which was logically safe but not frame-budget safe under hostile clustering.

Solution: Added `MaxSpatialHashChainSteps = 64`, rejected insertions beyond the chain cap, incremented `CounterSpatialHashOverflow`, and capped solver traversal with the same bound.

Rejected Alternatives: Increasing bucket count alone was rejected because collisions still need a deterministic hard stop. Sorting every frame was rejected for this pass because it adds broader code and compile-wall risk.

Scalability potential: Low tier keeps deterministic capping. Higher tiers can raise the cap or add a sorted-cell pass after profiler proof.

Hardware Impact: Prevents pathological O(N^2) collision-chain behavior on mobile CPU. Exact microseconds require profiler/device evidence.

## Decision 003 - Dear-Lie Flora Field Instead Of Hot-Path Trig

Problem: `MockFloraSpawner.SampleFloraMass()` used `math.sin` from the boid solver hot path. That is unnecessary physical polish for a mock nutritional field.

Solution: Replaced sine with a deterministic triangle-wave/fract field keyed by local position and species hash.

Rejected Alternatives: Keeping sine was rejected because low-tier math LOD should use simple scalar fakes. A texture/LUT was rejected here because the code path already uses unmanaged math and has no texture dependency.

Scalability potential: Low/Middle use the triangle fake. High/Ultra visual flora density can still be improved by renderer-side effects without changing biomass truth.

Hardware Impact: Removes one trig evaluation per active boid feed sample. Exact microseconds are not claimed without Burst profiler data.

## Decision 004 - Explicit Telemetry Tail Padding

Problem: `ShinobuTelemetryEntry` declared `Size=64` but fields stopped at byte 59. The CLR would honor the size, but the forensic layout report needed an explicit visible tail field.

Solution: Added `Pad0` at byte offset 60 to make the 64-byte cache-line payload explicit.

Rejected Alternatives: Relying on implicit tail padding was rejected because the user explicitly requested byte-layout proof.

Scalability potential: No gameplay scalability change; telemetry remains a fixed 300-entry ring.

Hardware Impact: 0 runtime microseconds; improves layout auditability and ARM64 review quality.

## Decision 005 - AUP Authority Comes From PlayerStateSignal

Problem: The runtime was accepting `CameraPositionSignal.Position` as input for `_cameraAup` reconstruction. That signal is camera-local `float3`, not universe-authoritative state, and would reintroduce large-world float jitter.

Solution: Read `PlayerStateSignal.PositionAup` first and only use `CameraPositionSignal` for local camera position/forward vectors. `IsFiniteAup()` rejects invalid AUP rows before they become the flocking center.

Rejected Alternatives: `AbsoluteUniversePosition.FromRuntimePosition(signal.Position)` was rejected because it converts a local float into a fake absolute coordinate.

Scalability potential: Low/Middle/High/Ultra all keep the same authoritative double coordinate chain; visual tiers can vary render density without touching simulation truth.

Hardware Impact: No claimed microseconds. This prevents precision drift and downstream NaN repair work in large 100km worlds.

## Decision 006 - Macro Biomass Hash Index Instead Of N*S Scans

Problem: The cold macro pass used linear sector lookup per entity and linear carnivore scans per starving sector. That produced `EntityCount * SectorCount` and `SectorCount * EntityCount` behavior in the exact system meant to save CPU.

Solution: Reused vault-owned `ShinobuSpatialHashBucketHeads` and enlarged `ShinobuSpatialHashNext` to `entityCapacity + sectorCapacity`. During ColdTick, the buffers become a sector-hash index plus per-sector carnivore chains. Sector creation is monotonic and starvation tombstoning walks only carnivores already linked to that sector.

Rejected Alternatives: Private dictionaries/NativeMultiHashMap were rejected by H-Phi data sovereignty. Sorting sector keys every ColdTick was rejected as wider compile-wall risk for this pass.

Scalability potential: Low tiers keep the hard cap and deterministic chains. Higher tiers can increase sector capacity or feed richer visual biomass without changing the macro truth.

Hardware Impact: Static estimate only. Eliminates two macro-path scan products; exact microseconds require profiler capture after the external `LocRegistry.cs` compile wall is cleared.

## Decision 007 - Render Payload NaN Quarantine

Problem: Invalid entity math could still reach the render matrices because `BuildShinobuRenderPayloadJob` only checked active/hydrated flags.

Solution: Reject rows flagged `EntityFlagInvalidMath` and finite-check position, velocity, biomass, and the final TRS matrix before writing BRG payload data.

Rejected Alternatives: Letting GPU consumers discover bad matrices was rejected because one NaN can poison culling/render paths.

Scalability potential: Low tiers avoid expensive recovery from broken render payloads. Higher tiers keep the same gameplay truth while consuming safe matrices for denser visuals.

Hardware Impact: Adds bounded scalar checks per render row; prevents catastrophic NaN propagation. No microsecond claim without profiler proof.

## Decision 008 - Blackbox Dump Format v2

Problem: The dump header declared a 64-byte telemetry entry, but the writer emitted only 60 bytes because `Pad0` was omitted. The telemetry cursor also normalized after index calculation.

Solution: Dump version moved to 2, the header writes `UnsafeUtility.SizeOf<ShinobuTelemetryEntry>()`, `Pad0` is serialized, and cursor overflow is normalized before selecting the ring slot.

Rejected Alternatives: Leaving implicit padding out of the file was rejected because post-mortem tooling would desynchronize entry boundaries.

Scalability potential: No visual-tier impact; the blackbox remains a fixed 300-frame ring.

Hardware Impact: Fault-path only. Main-frame cost is unchanged outside crash/overflow conditions.

